<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>How contents claims get short-changed</h2>
<p>After a fire or major water loss, the carrier or its vendor typically prices your inventory using a database of comparable items. The problems are rarely dramatic; they compound. A hand-made sofa is matched to a mass-market one. A ten-year-old item gets 90% depreciation. A whole category is depreciated at the same rate regardless of condition. Contents claim appraisal works through the list and corrects the pricing and depreciation where it’s wrong.</p>
<h2>What we review</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Like kind and quality:</strong> brand, materials and features should match what you had, not just the category.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation:</strong> rates should reflect the item’s actual condition and useful life. Some items, like antiques and fine art, may not depreciate at all.</li>
<li><strong>Sales tax and delivery:</strong> often omitted from replacement prices.</li>
<li><strong>Missed items:</strong> garages, attics, closets and outdoor areas are commonly under-inventoried.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Replacement cost and holdback</h2>
<p>With replacement cost coverage, the carrier usually pays actual cash value first and the depreciation once you replace the item. California law sets minimum time periods to collect that holdback, and longer periods after declared disasters. Our <a href="/claim-check-tools/acv-holdback-calculator/">ACV and holdback calculator</a> shows what you should expect at each stage.</p>
<h2>Building an inventory after a total loss</h2>
<p>Start room by room. Use phone photos, social media, bank and credit card statements, online order histories and family memories. Group small items logically. After a declared disaster, carriers in California may accept less detailed inventories for part of the contents limit, but a detailed list remains the most reliable way to support the full value.</p>
<h3>Special limits</h3>
<p>Jewelry, firearms, cash, collectibles and business property at home often carry low sublimits unless scheduled. Appraisal can’t increase those limits, but it can make sure what’s within them is valued correctly.</p>

<h2>Questions about contents &amp; personal property appraisal</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary>Can contents disputes be appraised?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Yes. If the carrier accepts the contents loss but disputes values or depreciation, appraisal can resolve the amounts.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I need receipts for everything?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>No. Receipts help, but photos, statements and reasonable descriptions are often enough, especially for ordinary household items.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is a reasonable depreciation rate?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>It depends on the item and its condition. Flat rates applied to whole categories are a common source of underpayment.</p></div></details></div>
